today a new study warned that humans  could be responsible for bringing about  the sixth mass extinction a marine life  now the study authors looked at the five  previous times that there's been a mass  extinction and they compared it to  what's taking place in the oceans today  and they found that the bigger the  animals are the greater their risk of  extinction so around 250 million years  ago the end permian mass extinction  wiped out around ninety five percent of  marine life but species size wasn't a  big factor in whether or not they were  likely to survive it also wasn't a big  factor during the n cretaceous period  which happened 65 million years ago and  that killed off the dinosaurs but the  author's studied 2497 ancient and modern  marine species and they found that over  the past 65 million years size has  become the biggest factor in how likely  a marine mammal is becoming extinct now  let's have a look in a bit more detail  here because if you look at forty  percent of marine species longer than a  metre are endangered that's like the  great white sharks for example and it  increases to eighty-six percent if the  mammal is over ten meters long and the  blue whale which is the largest sea  mammal has seen as much as a ninety  percent reduction in its population now  by comparison the bioluminescent bristle  mouth which measure just three inches  across they have a population of  hundreds of trillions scientists say  that this is a pattern that's been seen  before so on land for example the  extinction of big mammals like the  mammoth and also the giant ground sloth  over the past sixty six million years is  also thought to have been caused by  human hunting so big animals big chance  a big risk of extinction
 

